{My opponent won the Best Game Prize for this. Unfortunately it was subsequently discovered that there were numerous inaccuracies by both players. (MJY)} 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 h6 {Alexander was very fond of this move, and I never found any way of taking advantage of it. Black gets into the Classical Defence without giving White the option of the Kieseritsky. I do not share the Korchnoi/Zak view that the idea is ill founded (SMB)} 4. Bc4 (4. d4 g5 5. Nc3 d6 6. g3 {is probably the best way of making this line playable. (MJY)}) 4... d6 5. d4 g5 6. O-O Bg7 7. g3 Nc6 8. c3 Bh3 9. Re1 {The text move leads (especially after White's 10th move) to an extremely critical and double-edged position, like variations of the Steinitz Gambit. (SMB)} ({Korchnoi/Zak recommend the 1923 Spielmann exchange sacrifice} 9. gxf4 Bxf1 10. Qxf1 $44 {as giving White sufficient compensation; but I doubt it, particularly against} g4 $17 {with which Alexander once demolished me.} 11. Kh1 Nge7 12. Nbd2 Ng6 13. f5 b5 14. Be6 d5 15. f6 Na5 16. Nh4 b4) (9. Rf2 {is more than adequately met by} Nf6 {(SMB)}) 9... Qd7 {Not for the first time, I seem to have rapidly acquired a lost position. (MJY)} 10. gxf4 {A very courageous decision, but it is difficult otherwise to find a satisfactory plan. (SMB)} Qg4+ (10... gxf4 11. Kh1 Nge7 12. Bxf4 O-O-O 13. Nbd2 Ng6 14. Bg3 f5 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Bb5 Qf7 17. Qb3 Qxb3 18. axb3 Kb8 19. Bxc6 bxc6 20. Nc4 Rde8 21. Nfd2 h5 22. Rf1 Be6 23. h4 Bd5+ 24. Kh2 Re2+ 25. Rf2 Rhe8 26. Raf1 Bf6 27. Na5 Nxh4 28. c4 Nf3+ 29. Nxf3 Bxf3 30. Be5 Bxe5+ 31. dxe5 Rxf2+ 32. Rxf2 Rxe5 33. b4 Re2 34. Nxc6+ Kb7 35. Rxe2 Bxe2 {0-1, Yeo,M-Bellin,R, Mitcham v Atheneum 1977}) 11. Kf2 Qg2+ 12. Ke3 gxf4+ (12... Nf6 13. Kd3 Ng4 14. Qe2 {is no more convincing. White has recovered his pawn, and may well emerge with a very strong centre. (SMB)}) 13. Kd3 ({Of course not} 13. Kxf4 $2 h5 $19 {. But now Black finds he has enduring problems on the g-file. (SMB)}) 13... Bg4 14. Rg1 $1 Qh3 (14... Qxf3+ 15. Qxf3 Bxf3 16. Rxg7 Nf6 17. Rxf7 Bxe4+ 18. Kd2 Na5 $17 {MJY}) 15. Nbd2 Nge7 {Hoping to safeguard both his f-pawn and his g-file by ...Ng6. (SMB)} 16. Qf1 Qxf1+ (16... Qh5 $15 {MJY}) 17. Nxf1 $1 ({Again much better than} 17. Rxf1 {, after which} Ng6 {makes all safe. Now Black has to play very energetically to avoid coming out with the worst of it. (SMB)}) 17... f5 18. Nh4 {Another very ingenious resource. The white king is drawn forward, but bears a charmed life. (SMB)} ({MJY gives the text a '?' analysing} 18. Bxf4 fxe4+ 19. Kc2 h5 20. h3 Bxf3 (20... Bxh3 21. Ng5 Bg4 22. Ne3 Bh6 23. Nf7 Bxf4 24. Nxg4 hxg4 25. Nxh8 Kd7 26. Rxg4 Rxh8 27. Rxf4 Rh2+ 28. Kd1 $13) 21. Rxg7 Rf8 $15 {(MJY)}) (18. exf5 Bxf5+ 19. Ke2 Ng6 20. Bd3 Bxd3+ 21. Kxd3 Nce7 $15 {(MJY)}) 18... fxe4+ 19. Kxe4 d5+ 20. Bxd5 ({Not} 20. Kxf4 $2 h5 $17 {, followed by 0-0+. (SMB)}) 20... Nxd5 21. Kxd5 (21. Rxg4 Nf6+ 22. Kxf4 Nxg4 23. Kxg4 O-O $17 {(MJY)}) 21... O-O-O+ 22. Ke4 h5 {It really looks all over now but not a bit of it. (SMB)} ({"I have been delighted by your game ... Yeo-Milner-Barry and by the fair annotations from the winner. It seems, however, that Black can win, in a very beautiful manner, by playing} 22... Nxd4 $1 {instead of 22...h5, and now the best try for White would be} 23. Rxg4 (23. cxd4 $4 Rxd4#) (23. Kxf4 $2 Ne2+ {followed by 24...Nxg1 and wins}) (23. Bxf4 Rhe8+ 24. Kd3 Nb3+ 25. Kc4 (25. Kc2 Nxa1+ {and mate}) 25... Be6+ 26. Kb4 Bf8+ {, etc}) 23... Rhe8+ 24. Kd3 (24. Kxf4 Be5+ 25. Ke4 Bh8+ {is still worse}) 24... Nb3+ 25. Kc4 Nxa1 $1 (25... Na5+ {looks promising but I don't see a clear way to victory}) 26. Rxg7 Rd1 27. Bxf4 Re4+ {and wins (28 Kc5 Rxf4 29 Rg8 Rd8)." IM Ricardo Calvo in Palma de Mallorca, BCM, August 1978, p369}) 23. Kxf4 $1 ({Against the natural} 23. Bxf4 {,} Nxd4 $19 {is decisive (} 24. cxd4 Rxd4+ 25. Ke3 Re8+ {). But now the outcome is far from clear. (SMB)}) 23... Nxd4 ({Possibly} 23... Bh6+ 24. Kg3 Rdg8 $5 {was a better way to continue the attack. I took it for granted that there must be a forced win after 23...Nxd4. (SMB)}) ({MJY gives the text as '?!' analysing} 23... Rhg8 24. Rxg4 Be5+ 25. Ke3 Rxg4 26. Nf3 Bd6 $19 {(MJY)}) (23... Rdf8+ 24. Ke3 Bf6 25. Ng2 (25. Ng6 Re8+ 26. Kf4 Rhg8 $19) 25... Rhg8 26. Kd3 Bf5+ 27. Ke2 Bxd4 28. cxd4 Nxd4+ 29. Kf2 Bg4+ 30. Nf4 Ne6 31. Rg2 Nxf4 32. Bxf4 Rxf4+ 33. Kg1 Re8 $19 {(MJY)}) 24. cxd4 Rxd4+ 25. Kg3 Rd1 {Again White seems in extremis, since he has no prospect of developing his queenside pieces. (SMB)} 26. h3 $1 {But White still has a shot left in his locker, and a much better one than either of us realised.} (26. Kg2 Bf6 27. Ne3 Rxg1+ 28. Kxg1 Bxh4 29. Nxg4 Rg8 30. Bd2 Rxg4+ $17 {(MJY)}) 26... Be5+ (26... Be6 $19 {(MJY)}) 27. Kg2 ({Not} 27. Kf2 Rf8+ 28. Kg2 Be2) 27... Be2 28. Kf2 $2 ({As anticipated, but neither of us saw} 28. Ng6 $1 Rg8 {and only then} 29. Kf2 {, which leaves Black nothing better than} Rxg6 30. Rxg6 ({forced, for} 30. Kxe2 Rxg1 31. Kxd1 Rxf1+ {wins easily (SMB)}) 30... Bxf1 {When analysing this position afterwards I had come to the conclusion that Black could not lose and might well still win, and that I need not therefore be too ashamed of the best game prize that was awarded me at the prize-giving. But suddenly I observed to my horror} 31. Rg8+ Kd7 32. Rd8+ $1 Kxd8 33. Bg5+ Ke8 34. Rxd1 Bxh3 35. Re1 $18 {and the dog it was that died. (SMB)}) 28... Bxf1 29. Rxf1 Rf8+ 30. Bf4 ({The ending after} 30. Nf3 Rxf3+ 31. Kxf3 Rxf1+ $19 {is equally hopeless (SMB)}) 30... Rxf4+ 31. Nf3 Rxa1 32. Rxa1 Bxb2 33. Rb1 {A few more moves for fun. (SMB)} Bf6 34. Rb5 h4 35. Ke3 Ra4 {(n.b. Martin & O'Brien booklet stops here - JS)} 36. Rf5 Be7 37. Re5 Bd6 38. Re8+ Kd7 39. Rh8 Bg3 40. Rh7+ Kd6 41. Rh6+ Kc5 42. Rh5+ Kb6 43. Nd4 Rxa2 44. Nf5 Rh2 45. Nxg3 Rxh3 {If I had realised that 28 Ng6 actually won for White I should not have entered this game for the best game prize. But it was a stirring encounter all the same, and one to which my opponent fully contributed his share. Alexander would have enjoyed it. [Notes by the late Sir Stuart Milner-Barry, BCM, Jan 1978, p4; by Michael J Yeo in his '70 Memorable Games' collection; and by Ricardo Calvo, BCM, August 1978, p369]} 0-1

